    "description": "Translation connects Persian publishing with readers in other languages and brings foreign-language works into Persian. Literary translation involves editorial judgment, attribution, publication arrangements, and the technical presentation of text.",
    "tags": ["Translation", "Persian literature", "Literary publishing", "Localization", "Editorial practice"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T09:00:02Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T09:00:02Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Translation and Persian Digital Publishing | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand literary translation, Iranian book grants, editorial responsibilities, and Persian text production."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "translation-and-publication",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Translation as an editorial activity",
        "content": "In publishing, translation produces a work that readers can encounter in another language. The translator's contribution includes choices about voice, terminology, and meaning, while publication introduces further questions about editing, attribution, and permitted uses. The European Council of Literary Translators' Associations, known as CEATL, treats translators as creative participants whose work requires clear agreements and a collaborative editorial process. Its guidelines provide a professional reference point; they do not establish Iranian legal requirements. [s1]\n\nFor this page, the focus is literary and publishing translation. The evidence inspected does not support a directory of commercial agencies or a wider account of certified document translation, interpreting, and specialist technical services. Those activities should not be assumed to follow the same commissioning arrangements as a translated novel. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "outward-translation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Bringing Iranian books to other languages",
        "content": "Iran Book Grant describes a translation and publication program intended to support international publishers bringing Iranian books to readers abroad. Its website outlines registration by a foreign publisher, submission of book information, evaluation, an agreement following approval, and translation and international publication. The stated priorities include contemporary fiction and nonfiction, art, Iranian and Islamic studies, and children's and young adult literature. [s2]\n\nThis is evidence of a program's stated offer and application structure. It does not demonstrate how many applications succeed, which language markets receive support, or whether a particular translation has reached readers. Nor does a grant replace the distinct work of selecting a translator, preparing an edition, and arranging publication. A responsible account therefore separates the existence of support from verified evidence of completed and distributed books. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "editorial-responsibilities",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Editing, attribution, and changes to the text",
        "content": "CEATL's guidelines call for written agreements before work begins and for the rights licensed to be specified. They also emphasize translator attribution, participation in reviewing editorial changes, and sufficient time to produce a coherent work. These principles explain why translation quality involves more than a fluent sample: the final edition also depends on how revision and publication are handled. They are professional recommendations rather than individualized legal advice. [s1]\n\nThe consequences of changes are particularly visible in Akram Pedramnia's December 2019 essay for English PEN. The Iranian-Canadian translator described producing a Persian version of James Joyce's Ulysses outside Iran's domestic censorship system. She reported that its first volume had been published in the United Kingdom and discussed the distortion she believed would result from removing its sexual, political, and religious material. This is a translator's first-person account of a particular project and position, not a comprehensive survey of every translated book published in Iran. [s3]\n\nHer account also shows why the place of publication matters. A Persian-language edition produced abroad can have a different editorial history from one prepared for domestic authorization. The language of a book alone does not reveal whether it is complete, abridged, or altered. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "digital-handoff",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From translated manuscript to readable edition",
        "content": "Digital preparation introduces another layer of work. Persian uses an Arabic-derived script with right-to-left text, while numbers and embedded Latin writing introduce different directional behavior. W3C's Arabic and Persian layout requirements document these issues for web content and digital publications. A translated manuscript can therefore require language editing and layout review as separate checks: accurate wording does not by itself establish correct on-screen presentation. [s4]\n\nFor readers, a useful edition makes its translator visible and its editorial scope understandable. For researchers, the important distinction is between a program's promises, a translator's account, professional guidance, and evidence about the finished publication. Each answers a different question. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
