    "description": "Writing tools for Persian digital publishing address composition, spacing, text processing, and the display of mixed-direction content. Persian-specific language software complements editors and publishing systems, but automated processing does not establish factual or editorial accuracy.",
    "tags": ["Persian writing", "Text editing", "Typography", "Natural language processing", "Digital publishing"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T09:00:02Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T09:00:02Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Persian Writing Tools and Text Preparation | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Persian spacing, bidirectional text, Hazm language processing, and the limits of automated writing support."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "persian-text",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why Persian writing needs specific support",
        "content": "Persian digital writing presents challenges that a generic text box does not explain. Letters join according to their context, text normally runs from right to left, and a paragraph may contain Latin names, numerical expressions, or technical abbreviations. W3C's Arabic and Persian layout requirements describe these as matters for web and digital-publication technology. The document is a Group Draft Note, so it should be read as technical guidance rather than a certification that a particular editor handles every case correctly. [s1]\n\nCharacter selection matters as well as appearance. W3C's tables distinguish characters used in Persian from related Arabic forms, including Persian keheh and Farsi yeh. This helps explain why visually similar text can contain different underlying characters. A useful editorial check therefore considers the stored text as well as what a font makes it look like. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "spacing",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Spaces and the half-space",
        "content": "One important Persian text feature is the zero-width non-joiner, commonly called a half-space. It prevents joining without inserting an ordinary visible word space. Research on PerSpaCor identifies space and non-joiner errors as a distinct correction problem affecting Persian readability and textual accuracy. Its authors describe transformer-based models trained to correct these errors at character level. [s2]\n\nThat research is relevant because writing assistance can target a precise problem rather than attempt to rewrite an entire passage. However, the inspected abstract establishes the study's task and reported approach, not the reliability of an installed product in every editorial setting. Its findings should not be generalized into a claim that automatic correction can safely resolve every ambiguity in an author's prose. Full methodological assessment would require inspection of the paper beyond the abstract. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "language-processing",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Language processing behind editorial tools",
        "content": "Hazm is a Python toolkit for processing Persian text. Its project documentation lists normalization, sentence and word tokenization, lemmatization, grammatical tagging, dependency analysis, and other functions. Normalization makes selected text conventions more consistent; tokenization divides text into units such as words or sentences; lemmatization associates word forms with a dictionary form. These capabilities are building blocks for software rather than a complete publishing workflow. [s3]\n\nFor example, an editorial system might use normalization before indexing articles or use sentence boundaries when organizing text for further analysis. Those are possible applications of the documented functions, not claims that a named Iranian publisher has adopted them. Nor does grammatical tagging establish that an article's argument, quotation, or historical statement is correct. The distinction between processing language and verifying content remains essential. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "mixed-direction",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Checking the published result",
        "content": "Mixed-direction text needs attention after composition. W3C's guidance explains that an embedded phrase can affect adjacent punctuation or numbers unless its direction is appropriately isolated. It distinguishes document and block direction, which require markup, from inline plain-text situations where Unicode controls can help. Its examples show why an English abbreviation inside a Persian title can appear in the wrong position. [s4]\n\nThe editorial implication is to review text in its destination, including titles and other short fields, rather than assuming that a clean manuscript guarantees a clean display. Spacing correction, language processing, and directional layout address different layers of the same publication. A useful writing environment supports those layers while leaving decisions about meaning, evidence, and voice with an accountable editor. [s2][s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
